    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "Margaret McLeod Williams (1875–1957) and Harry S. Williams (1876–1942), Cincinnati, OH; by descent to their son, Murdoch M. Williams (1910–1978), Yellow Springs, OH, by 1957 to at least 1972 [Cincinnati, 1972, cat. 35]; possibly with DeVant Crissey Gallery, Smyrna, GA, c. 1976 [Ketner 1993, 205 no. 157]; by descent to his sister, Margaret Williams Rouse (1917–2004), Cincinnati, OH, from 1978; estate of Margaret Williams Rouse, Cincinnati, OH, by 2006 [this and the following according to emails from Paige Kestenman, Christie’s, and Katie Benedict, Hindman Auctions, Jul. 5, 2023; copies in curatorial object file]; offered for sale, Christie’s, New York, May 25, 2006, lot 107, bought in; offered for sale, Cowan’s Auction, Cincinnati, Oct. 17, 2008, lot 25, bought in. Private collection, Louisiana [according to email from Sophie Hirabayashi, Neal Auction Company, Jul. 12, 2023; copy in curatorial object file]; sold, Neal Auction Company, New Orleans, LA, Apr. 21, 2012, lot 333, as <em>Ohio River Valley Landscape</em> to Arader Galleries, New York [invoice; copy in curatorial object file]; sold to the Art Institute of Chicago, 2023.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435438",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Provenance Statement",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "<p>Robert S. Duncanson featured passages of open brushwork and luminous reflections along the water in this immersive landscape. Amid the autumnal foliage at center, two figures walk along the shore while another pair readies a boat in the foreground.</p>\n<p>A third-generation free person of color, Duncanson established himself as an artist in Cincinnati in the 1840s, garnering national and international acclaim in the following decades. Working at the border of North and South, he fled the heightened racial inequities of the US for Canada in 1861, later working in Britain. After the Civil War he returned to Cincinnati, painting <em>River Scene</em> during this late moment in his career.</p>\n",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416",
